{"name":"Peacock Plume","hex":"#206d71","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peacock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A male peafowl, especially Pavo cristatus, notable for its brilliant iridescently ocellated tail.","A peafowl (of the genus Pavo or Afropavo), either male or female."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To strut about proudly or haughtily.","To engage in peacocking, ostentatious dress or behaviour to impress women."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peacock"},{"term":"plume","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feather of a bird, especially a large or showy one used as a decoration.","A cluster of feathers worn as an ornament, especially on a helmet; a hackle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To adorn, cover, or furnish with feathers or plumes, or as if with feathers or plumes.","Chiefly of a bird: to arrange and preen the feathers of, specifically in preparation for flight; hence , to prepare for (something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plume"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plume (feather)","blocks":[{"text":"A plume is a special type of bird feather, possessed by egrets, ostriches, birds of paradise, quetzals, pheasants, peacocks and quails. They often have a decorative or ornamental purpose, commonly used among marching bands and the military, worn on the hat or helmet of the wearer. Famously by the blues & royals and the Life Guards. When used on military headdresses, the clipped feather plume is referred to as the hackle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Cavalier_Garde_R%C3%A9publicaine_trois-quart_dos.jpg/330px-Cavalier_Garde_R%C3%A9publicaine_trois-quart_dos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plume_(feather)","wikidata":"Q7205412"},{"title":"Peafowl","blocks":[{"text":"Peafowl is a common name for two bird species of the genus Pavo and one species of the closely related genus Afropavo within the tribe Pavonini of the family Phasianidae. Male peafowl are referred to as peacocks, and female peafowl are referred to as peahens. Despite this, peacock is usually used to refer to both sexes, in common language."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Peacock_Plumage.jpg/330px-Peacock_Plumage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peafowl","wikidata":"Q201251"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg/500px-Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg/500px-Eggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEggs_of_Peafowl_at_Aravath_Kasaragod.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/500px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/1280px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg/500px-Baby_Peacock_%2818131813108%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaby_Peacock_(18131813108).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Peacock-JS.jpg/500px-Peacock-JS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia